# CoW invariant: `String s = vec.get(i).unwrap()` borrow held across
# `vec.push(...)` was silently invalidated by realloc.
#
# Pre-fix (2026-05-13 follow-up to the `expand_derives` for-loop
# investigation): the safety pass DID detect this pattern and emit a
# `CowBorrowMutation` warning ("`v` mutated while `s` holds an element
# — clone is inserted automatically"), but the actual clone was placed
# INSIDE the loop body via `cow_materialize_collection_ref`. The
# rebinding of `s` → owned_local didn't survive the enclosing loop's
# `save_locals`/`restore_locals` boundary, so post-loop instructions
# still referenced the original Ptr-typed local pointing into the
# freed buffer.
#
# Manifested as a heap-fragmentation-threshold UAF: small inputs
# realloc in-place (buffer stays, stale-but-valid pointer), large
# inputs force buffer moves (stale-and-invalid). The self-host's
# `loader.gg::load_imports` hit this on httpserver-router imports.
#
# Fix: at the var-decl site in `lower_var_decl`, check
# `is_source_mut_unsafe_at(source_path, stmt_span_start)`. If the
# source collection is mutated later, skip the CowBorrow propagation
# and fall through to the eager-clone branch. The destination becomes
# an owned String at the var-decl site, safely outliving any
# subsequent mutation. Mirrors the `mod_path.clone()` defensive
# pattern users would otherwise write by hand.
void main():
    Vector[String] v = ["hello", "world"]
    String s = v.get(0).unwrap()
    int i = 0
    while i < 20:
        v.push("filler")
        i = i + 1
    print(f"s = {s}")
    print(f"v[0] = {v.get(0).unwrap()}")
    print(f"v.len() = {v.len()}")
